# Work-Life balance

When I was in school, the weighing scales were analogue.

You would put stuff on one side, weights on the other and try to balance.

I remember there were a few puzzles of 8 identical coins, one of which had a different weight, and you had to find out which one was different in the minimum number of tries.

As I moved into corporate, the balance scale had a different reference.

*Work-Life balance!!!*

Articles have been written on the adverse effects of a poor work-life balance, how to manage it and how you need to get a life! I am sure there are books out there trying to teach you the 'How'.

Despite all that, I did go through sustained periods when the balance was out of sync.

The advent of laptops, phones (BlackBerry - hope you remember this one!) and smartphones made me more accessible.

It also made my work more accessible to me.

Instead of helping to bring the balance, it caused work to creep onto the dining table.

In the digital age, the balance itself became digital.

Now, you could not reduce work from one side of the balance to bring life in line.

All the work, life, health and other priorities were stacked one over the other on a digital scale, with a flashing red display.

The only way to bring the balance was to remove one, so that the number on the scale looked less scary.

It has taken quite a brave decision, and a few sacrifices, for me to keep it in sync.

The number on the digital weighing scale looks more manageable now.

Every now and then, some work becomes heavy, causing me to take off other important things.

I am trying to limit those days and ensure I put the other things back when the work is addressed.

I would like to hear from you how you are managing this!
